; A tall climb: four narrow ledges staggered left/right above a short
; ground strip, topped by a wider summit. Rewards vertical swing play
; instead of the ground-level scrapping a flat stage encourages.
;
; The third ledge crumbles (issue #18). A climb whose every rung is
; permanent rewards getting up first and then holding the summit, because
; the route that got you there is still sitting under you, doing nothing
; for your opponent that it did not already do for you. Making the middle
; rung go away under the first player to use it means the climb is not the
; same climb twice: whoever goes up first spends the route, and whoever
; follows either waits the three seconds out or finds another way.
;
; The third rung specifically, not the first or the summit. The first rung
; is the only way off the ground, so crumbling it could strand a player
; with nothing to do but be hit; the summit is the prize and should not
; evaporate under the player who earned it. The middle of a climb is the
; one place a closing route costs position rather than the round.
;
; Eased for issue #59, because every rung was a long reach for anything
; short of the staff: 200 px ledges with 200-270 px of air between their
; inner edges, so each hand-off was 233-295 px corner to corner. Every
; ledge is now 240 wide and sits closer to the middle -- Ledge1 x -250 ->
; -190, Ledge2 220 -> 170, Ledge3 -180 -> -170, Ledge4 220 -> 170 --
; so the gaps between inner edges are 100-120 px and the hand-offs
; 156-170 px corner to corner. Ledge4 also dropped 10 (y -100 -> -90) and
; the Summit 20 (y -260 -> -240), taking those two climbs from 140 to 130
; and 160 to 150 px. Still a
; left/right stagger, still four rungs and a summit, still the third one
; crumbles. A headless search over arm drags (not committed) found every
; rung climbable with the pickaxe afterwards, and more forgiving of a
; sloppy thumb: under the same jitter, Ledge2 -> Ledge3 went from 9 to 32
; successes in 60, Ledge3 -> Ledge4 8 -> 20, Ledge4 -> Summit 14 -> 23,
; and the dagger could now make Ledge1 -> Ledge2 and Ledge3 -> Ledge4,
; which it could not before. The sword still cannot leave flat ground
; here, as on every step-up this search tried from a floor -- it barely
; lifts itself, so no rung spacing fixes that.

; Backdrop (issue #117): city at night.
